<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transcaspian Oblast</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Transcaspia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Transcaspian Oblast was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, roughly corresponding to modern Turkmenistan, formed after the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> in 1881 and enlarged by the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> in 1884. Its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashgabat">Ashgabat</a>, and the Trans-Caspian railway crossed it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenbasy">Krasnovodsk</a>. In 1918–1919 it was held by an anti-Bolshevik government, and in 1924 it became the core of the Turkmen SSR.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transcaspian-oblast.jpg" alt="Transcaspian Oblast" width="320" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Transcaspia_in_Russian_Empire_(1914).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
